<!-- ============ CodeJitsu ============ -->
<section class="section" id="codejitsu" aria-labelledby="codejitsu-title">
    <div class="container">
        <header class="section-header">
            <p class="eyebrow">01 · Case study</p>
            <h2 class="section-header__title" id="codejitsu-title">CodeJitsu: an AI coach that watches BJJ sparring footage</h2>
            <p class="section-header__description">
                Upload a Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu sparring video and get a timestamped match breakdown, a technical grade
                and the drills that fix what went wrong. The same weaknesses then feed AI-drafted class plans and
                partner pairings for the next session.
            </p>
        </header>

        <figure class="figure figure--hero fade-up">
            <img src="~/images/portfolio/codejitsu-hero.webp" alt="CodeJitsu landing page: &quot;AI that reads a BJJ sparring tape&quot;" width="1568" height="372" fetchpriority="high" />
        </figure>

        <div class="zen-divider"><span>At a glance</span></div>

        <dl class="kv-panel">
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>Solo builder: product, architecture, development and operations</dd>
            <dt>Timeline</dt>
            <dd>Hand-built side project from February 2024; developed with a team of AI agents since March 2026</dd>
            <dt>Company</dt>
            <dd>Free demo and internal R&amp;D tool at <a href="https://tknq.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TKNQ</a>, where I'm CTO</dd>
            <dt>Stack</dt>
            <dd>Gemini on Vertex AI · .NET 10 microservices · React 19 · Supabase PostgreSQL · Google Cloud · Cloudflare</dd>
            <dt>Live</dt>
            <dd><a href="https://thecodejitsu.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">thecodejitsu.com</a></dd>
        </dl>

        <div class="prose">
            <h3>The problem</h3>
            <p>
                A five-minute roll holds dozens of transitions, scrambles and positions that look alike. Coaches can't
                rewatch every round for every student, so detailed feedback rarely happens. Can a multimodal model act
                as a reliable second set of eyes for an instructor, and turn that feedback into the next class?
            </p>
            <p>
                It's also a hard benchmark for video AI: long footage, fast action, and a half guard that looks almost
                the same as a quarter guard. That's why TKNQ keeps CodeJitsu running as its R&amp;D testbed.
            </p>

            <h3>Design decisions</h3>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Analysis runs as a background job</strong> (Hangfire on Postgres), with progress pushed to the browser over SignalR.</li>
                <li><strong>A person stays in the loop.</strong> Instructors can edit every AI output before a student sees it.</li>
                <li><strong>Model choice is a setting, not code.</strong> An admin AI Console sets the model, region, reasoning level and token limit per feature, and every run snapshots its settings, so a new Gemini generation is a configuration change instead of a redeploy.</li>
                <li><strong>Knowing when not to use AI.</strong> Technique search first used Grok live search (dead links), then Gemini with Google Search grounding (too slow for the server's time limit). A plain YouTube Data API search replaced both: instant, cheap, and only real videos.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="grid grid--2 u-mt-6">
            <figure class="figure">
                <img src="~/images/portfolio/codejitsu-class-curriculum.webp" alt="AI-drafted 60-minute class plan split into warm-up, techniques, drills, sparring and cool-down" width="1053" height="856" loading="lazy" />
                <figcaption>Weaknesses found in students' videos feed an AI-drafted plan for the next class.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <figure class="figure">
                <img src="~/images/portfolio/codejitsu-partner-pairs.webp" alt="AI partner pairing: four pairs with the model's written reasoning on belt, experience, weight, height and age" width="1073" height="600" loading="lazy" />
                <figcaption>A matchmaking service pairs training partners by belt, weight and experience, and explains its reasoning.</figcaption>
            </figure>
        </div>

        <div class="prose">
            <h3>Running a paid AI feature as a free demo</h3>
            <p>Every analysis costs real money, so the controls had to be fair to users and safe for the budget:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>3 analyses per account per rolling 24 hours, adjustable live from the AI Console.</li>
                <li>Failed runs are refunded automatically, and automatic job retries are off. Before that change, one failure could quietly re-run the whole paid analysis up to ten times.</li>
                <li>The limit is checked before upload, two requests can't both take the last slot, and a double-click never costs two runs.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3>Built with a team of AI agents</h3>
            <p>
                Since March 2026, CodeJitsu has been developed with Claude Code working as five specialised agents,
                each defined by a role file in the repository. I set direction, answer the product questions and
                review what ships.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="table-wrap">
            <table class="zen-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr><th scope="col">Agent</th><th scope="col">Responsibility</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Project manager</td><td>Breaks work into tasks, delegates, reviews code, enforces quality checks</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Frontend developer</td><td>React and TypeScript UI, state, English and Polish translations</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Backend developer</td><td>.NET APIs, domain services, EF Core models and migrations</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>DevOps engineer</td><td>Docker, Google Cloud, Nginx, CI/CD and deployments</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>QA tester</td><td>Writes tests, verifies changes, hunts edge cases</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <div class="zen-divider"><span>Results</span></div>

        <div class="stats">
            <div class="stat"><div class="stat__value">380</div><div class="stat__label">automated tests across backend and frontend</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="stat__value">5</div><div class="stat__label">specialised AI agents building the product</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="stat__value">0</div><div class="stat__label">redeploys needed to switch Gemini models</div></div>
            <div class="stat"><div class="stat__value">3/day</div><div class="stat__label">free analyses per account, with automatic refunds on failure</div></div>
        </div>

        <div class="scroll-card u-mt-6">
            <h3 class="scroll-card__title">What I learned</h3>
            <div class="scroll-card__body">
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Models change faster than code ships.</strong> Treating the model as a setting turned a breaking migration into a two-minute change.</li>
                    <li><strong>Not every feature needs a model.</strong> Technique search got faster, cheaper and more reliable without AI.</li>
                    <li><strong>Cost controls are part of the feature.</strong> Refunds, the in-progress guard and disabled retries belonged in version one.</li>
                    <li><strong>Agents speed up delivery, not judgment.</strong> Product decisions, reviews and approvals stayed my job, and that's where the quality came from.</li>
                </ol>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card__footer u-pt-5">
            <ul class="badge-list">
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Gemini</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Vertex AI</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Claude Code</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">.NET 10</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Hangfire</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">SignalR</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">React 19</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Supabase</li>
            </ul>
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="https://thecodejitsu.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try the live demo @Icons.Svg("arrow-up-right", 14)</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ============ JRE Clipper ============ -->
<section class="section section--surface" id="jre-clipper" aria-labelledby="jre-title">
    <div class="container">
        <header class="section-header">
            <p class="eyebrow">02 · Case study</p>
            <h2 class="section-header__title" id="jre-title">JRE Clipper: "What would Joe Rogan say?"</h2>
            <p class="section-header__description">
                A search engine for the Joe Rogan Experience that finds moments by what was said, not by which
                episode it was in.
            </p>
        </header>

        <div class="grid grid--2">
            <div class="scroll-card">
                <h3 class="scroll-card__title">The problem, as a fan</h3>
                <div class="scroll-card__body">
                    <p>
                        I've listened to the JRE for years. I'd remember Joe and a guest getting deep into a topic, like
                        jiu-jitsu, bears or psychedelics, and have no idea which episode it was, let alone where in a
                        three-hour conversation.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Keyword search doesn't help. Episode titles are mostly just the guest's name, and nobody
                        remembers the exact words that were used.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="scroll-card">
                <h3 class="scroll-card__title">My solution: search by meaning</h3>
                <div class="scroll-card__body">
                    <p>
                        I split the episode transcripts into short, timestamped segments and turned each one into a
                        vector embedding with Vertex AI, then indexed them all in Vertex AI Vector Search.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Your question is embedded the same way, so the closest segments come back by meaning even when
                        none of your words match. Each result is an embedded YouTube player that jumps straight to the
                        moment it was said.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card__footer u-pt-5">
            <ul class="badge-list">
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Data embeddings</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Semantic search</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Vertex AI Vector Search</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Python</li>
                <li class="badge badge--parch">Google Cloud</li>
            </ul>
            <a class="btn btn--primary" href="https://whatwouldjoerogansay.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Search the archive @Icons.Svg("arrow-up-right", 14)</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

